## One token per pass

A model writes one token per pass through all its blocks (Ch 11), and each pass has to read every
weight from memory. For a big model, most of the time goes into that reading, not the
arithmetic.<Cite id="leviathan2023" /> So a big model writing 30 tokens reads all its weights 30
times, even for the easy ones, like the next number in "one, two, three".

Here is the trick that helps. In one pass, a transformer doesn't only predict the token after the
last one: it predicts the next token after *every* position at once. That is how it reads a whole
prompt in one go. Reading a
few more tokens costs a big model about as much as reading one, because the weights are read once
either way.

## Guess, then check

So let a small, fast model guess. It writes the next few tokens on its own, cheaply. Then the big
model reads the text plus all the guesses in one pass, and at each position it compares its own top
token with the guess. It keeps the guesses up to the first one it disagrees with, swaps that one for
its own token, and drops the rest. If every guess matches, it adds one more token of its own. This
is <GlossaryPopover term="speculative-decoding">speculative decoding</GlossaryPopover>.<Cite id="leviathan2023" /><Cite id="chen2023" />
Below, GPT-2 small (124 million weights) guesses four tokens at a time for GPT-2 medium (355
million). Both use the same tokens, which the trick needs:

<SpeculativeStepper />

Every pass of the big model adds at least one token, its own, so it is never slower in passes than
writing alone. When the guesses are good it adds up to five. For "The cat sat on the", GPT-2 medium
wrote 30 tokens in 7 passes instead of 30. Counting ("six, seven, eight, …") is easy for the small
model too: 7 passes. "The capital of France is" took 9, because two of the small model's first
guesses were wrong.

And the text is the same, token for token, as GPT-2 medium writing alone. The big model checks every
token and only keeps a guess that is exactly what it would have picked. The drafter only changes how
many passes it takes. When the model samples instead of always picking the top token (Ch 2), a
slightly different rule accepts or replaces each guess at random so that every token still comes
out with exactly the big model's probabilities.<Cite id="leviathan2023" /><Cite id="chen2023" />

## In real models

On real hardware this makes big models write about two to three times faster with identical
output.<Cite id="leviathan2023" /><Cite id="chen2023" /> Some model families ship their own small
drafters. Google's Gemma 4 models have matching drafters named with `-assistant`, from 78 million
to half a billion weights, and the model card reports up to three times faster
writing.<Cite id="gemma4-assistant" /> They guess several tokens ahead for their big model, which
is why the card calls them multi-token prediction drafters.

<RecapCard>

- A big model spends most of each pass reading its weights, and one pass can check many tokens.
- In speculative decoding, a small model guesses a few tokens and the big model checks them all at
  once, keeping the ones it agrees with.
- Each pass adds at least one token, and the text is exactly what the big model would write alone.
- Real systems report writing about two to three times faster; Gemma 4 ships drafters named
  `-assistant`.

</RecapCard>

